// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Lesson 15.3: How writers transform their sources.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

const SOURCES: Q[] = [
  { prompt: 'In Ovid’s *Metamorphoses*, Pyramus and Thisbe are young lovers whose parents forbid their marriage. They whisper through a crack in a wall, and each dies believing the other dead. Which Shakespeare play tells a story of the same shape as a tragedy?', right: '*Romeo and Juliet*', wrong: ['*Macbeth*', '*The Tempest*', '*Julius Caesar*'], why: 'Forbidden love and a death caused by a mistaken belief link Ovid’s tale to *Romeo and Juliet* (RL.9-10.9).' },
  { prompt: 'In *A Midsummer Night’s Dream*, a group of tradesmen perform “Pyramus and Thisbe,” and one of them plays the Wall. How has Shakespeare transformed Ovid’s story here?', right: 'He turns a tragic myth into comedy by having amateurs stage it clumsily', wrong: ['He keeps every line of Ovid exactly as written, with no changes at all', 'He moves the story to Scotland and makes the lovers into a king and queen', 'He removes the lovers and keeps only the wall, as a symbol of division'], why: 'The same source becomes tragedy in one play and farce in another, depending on how the writer treats it (RL.9-10.9).' },
  { prompt: 'Shakespeare’s main source for *Romeo and Juliet* was a long poem by Arthur Brooke (1562) in which the story unfolds over about nine months. Shakespeare compresses it into a few days. What is the effect?', right: 'The rush of events makes the love seem more intense and the tragedy more hasty', wrong: ['It gives the lovers more time to plan carefully and avoid their mistakes', 'It shows that Shakespeare did not read the whole of Brooke’s poem', 'It makes the play longer than Brooke’s poem, so it could fill a day'], why: 'Changes to time and pace are a key way writers transform their source material (RL.9-10.9).' },
  { prompt: 'Mary Shelley subtitled *Frankenstein* “The Modern Prometheus.” What does the subtitle tell readers?', right: 'Victor, like the Titan who brought fire to humans, oversteps and is punished', wrong: ['The novel is set in ancient Greece among the gods of Mount Olympus', 'The creature is a god who has come down to Earth to help humanity', 'Shelley translated an old Greek play and gave it a modern ending'], why: 'A writer can signal a source and invite readers to compare the new work with the myth (RL.9-10.9).' },
  { prompt: 'Gonzalo’s speech about an ideal commonwealth in *The Tempest* draws on Montaigne’s essay “Of Cannibals.” Why does it matter that Shakespeare gives the idea to a kindly but mocked old courtier?', right: 'The play can explore the dream of a perfect society and question it at once', wrong: ['It proves that Shakespeare agreed with every idea in Montaigne’s essay', 'It shows that Gonzalo is the hero who rules the island at the end', 'It means the speech should be cut, since the idea came from elsewhere'], why: 'Writers transform a source by placing it in a new context and voice, which can endorse or test it (RL.9-10.9).' },
  { prompt: 'The musical *West Side Story* (1957) retells *Romeo and Juliet* with rival New York gangs. Which question best helps you analyse the transformation?', right: 'What does moving the feud to rival gangs show about prejudice?', wrong: ['How many songs does the musical include in its first act?', 'Which actors starred in the very first Broadway production?', 'Is the musical longer or shorter than the original play?'], why: 'Analysing a transformation means asking what the changes reveal, not just listing them (RL.9-10.9).' },
];

const TRANSFORM: Q[] = [
  { prompt: 'Which sentence **analyses** a writer’s transformation of a source, rather than just noting it?', right: 'By making Juliet thirteen instead of sixteen, Shakespeare sharpens our sense of her youth and haste.', wrong: ['Shakespeare used a poem by Arthur Brooke as the main source for his play about two lovers.', 'Juliet is thirteen in Shakespeare’s play and sixteen in Brooke’s poem, which came first.', 'Brooke’s poem is long and was published in 1562, many years before Shakespeare’s play.'], why: 'Analysis explains the effect of a change; noting a change is only the first step (RL.9-10.9).' },
  { prompt: 'A modern novel retells a Greek myth from the point of view of a minor female character who is silent in the original. What kind of transformation is this?', right: 'A shift in point of view that gives a voice to someone the source ignored', wrong: ['A translation that keeps the original’s words and its point of view', 'A summary that shortens the myth but changes none of its meaning', 'A parody that exaggerates the hero to make readers laugh at him'], why: 'Retelling from a new point of view is one of the most common ways writers transform sources (RL.9-10.9).' },
  { prompt: 'Prospero’s speech renouncing his magic in *The Tempest* echoes a witch’s spell in Ovid. What does this borrowing suggest about Prospero?', right: 'His power is darker and more dangerous than he admits', wrong: ['He has never practised any magic before the play', 'He is a comic figure whom the audience should laugh at', 'He is copying Ovid because he has run out of ideas'], why: 'An echo of a source can add a meaning the speaker does not state outright (RL.9-10.9).' },
  { prompt: 'Which pair of terms names two different ways writers draw on earlier works?', right: 'allusion and adaptation', wrong: ['metaphor and simile', 'rhyme and rhythm', 'stanza and verse'], why: 'An allusion briefly refers to a source; an adaptation retells it in a new form or setting.' },
  { prompt: 'A student writes: “Shakespeare stole his plots, so his plays are not original.” Which response is best supported?', right: 'Borrowing plots was normal then; originality lay in what he did with them.', wrong: ['That is right, since any writer who uses a source has done no real work.', 'That is wrong, since Shakespeare invented every one of his plots himself.', 'That does not matter, since nobody today can know which sources he used.'], why: 'Renaissance writers routinely reworked sources, and their art is in the transformation (RL.9-10.9).' },
  { prompt: 'What question should you ask first when comparing a work with its source?', right: 'What did the writer keep, what did they change, and why?', wrong: ['Which of the two works is older and more famous?', 'Which of the two works has more characters in it?', 'Which of the two works did I find easier to read?'], why: 'Comparing what is kept and changed, and asking why, is the core of analysing a transformation.' },
];

