// Lesson 15.4: World literature.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

const POSTMASTER = `From Rabindranath Tagore, “The Postmaster” (*Stories from Tagore*, 1918):

> The postmaster first took up his duties in the village of Ulapur. Though the village was a small one, there was an indigo factory near by, and the proprietor, an Englishman, had managed to get a post office established. Our postmaster belonged to Calcutta. He felt like a fish out of water in this remote village.`;

const BET = `From Anton Chekhov, “The Bet” (translated by Constance Garnett, 1921), the prisoner’s letter after nearly fifteen years alone:

> “To prove to you in action how I despise all that you live by, I renounce the two million of which I once dreamed as of paradise and which now I despise. To deprive myself of the right to the money I shall go out from here five hours before the time fixed, and so break the compact ...”`;

const WORLD: Q[] = [
  { prompt: `${POSTMASTER}\n\nWhat cultural experience does the opening set up?`, right: 'A man from Calcutta who feels out of place in a remote village', wrong: ['An English factory owner who longs to return to his own village', 'A village girl who dreams of becoming a postmaster in Calcutta', 'A traveller who is lost in the jungle and cannot find a village'], why: 'The story is built on the gap between the city-bred postmaster and village life in Bengal under British rule (RL.9-10.6).' },
  { prompt: `${POSTMASTER}\n\nWhat does the detail about the Englishman and the indigo factory tell us about the setting?`, right: 'The village lives under British rule, with a colonial business nearby', wrong: ['The village is in England, and its people grow indigo for sale', 'The postmaster owns the factory and employs the whole village', 'The story is set in the distant future, after a war has ended'], why: 'World literature asks readers to notice the history behind a setting, here colonial India (RL.9-10.6).' },
  { prompt: 'At the end of “The Postmaster,” the postmaster leaves, consoling himself with thoughts about life’s meetings and partings, while the orphan girl Ratan, who has looked after him, waits in tears for him to return. What does the contrast reveal?', right: 'He can step back into his wider world, but she has no such escape', wrong: ['He is heartless, and the story wants readers to hate him for it', 'She is foolish, and the story wants readers to laugh at her hope', 'They both feel exactly the same about the parting, and accept it'], why: 'The ending shows how class, age and place shape two people’s experience of one event (RL.9-10.6).' },
  { prompt: `${BET}\n\nWhy does the prisoner leave five hours early?`, right: 'To lose the money on purpose and prove he despises what it stands for', wrong: ['To claim the money sooner, before the banker can change his mind', 'To escape before the guards arrive and lock him up for life', 'To win a second bet with the banker about who was the cleverer'], why: 'He breaks the compact deliberately, rejecting the worldly goods the banker lives by.' },
  { prompt: `${BET}\n\nThe story began with a party debate over whether the death penalty or life in prison is more humane. What does the ending suggest about that question?`, right: 'Years of confinement changed the man so deeply that money lost its meaning', wrong: ['The death penalty is clearly kinder, and the banker was right all along', 'Fifteen years in prison has no effect on a person who is determined', 'The question was never important to the story and is soon forgotten'], why: 'Reading world literature closely means following how its ideas, here about freedom and punishment, develop (RL.9-10.6).' },
  { prompt: 'Why read stories such as Chekhov’s and Tagore’s in translation?', right: 'They show points of view and cultures from beyond the United States', wrong: ['Translations are always shorter, so they take less time to read', 'Stories in translation have no setting, so they are easier to follow', 'Only translated stories are allowed in high school English classes'], why: 'The standards ask for a point of view or cultural experience in literature from outside the United States (RL.9-10.6).' },
];

const WORLD_CONTEXT: Q[] = [
  { prompt: 'Chinua Achebe’s novel *Things Fall Apart* (1958) follows Okonkwo, a proud Igbo leader, as British missionaries and colonial rule arrive in his region of Nigeria. What point of view does the novel give that many earlier English novels about Africa did not?', right: 'The life of an African community, told from within it before and during colonial rule', wrong: ['The adventures of British explorers who travel across Africa for the first time', 'The view of a colonial governor who writes reports about the people he rules', 'The diary of a missionary who explains Igbo customs to readers in England'], why: 'Achebe wrote partly to answer European portrayals of Africa, telling the story from an Igbo point of view (RL.9-10.6).' },
  { prompt: 'Why should a reader learn something about Russia in the 1880s before reading “The Bet”?', right: 'The setting shapes the characters, such as a rich banker and a young lawyer', wrong: ['Without it, the story’s plot would be impossible to follow at all', 'Chekhov wrote only for Russian readers, so no one else can enjoy it', 'The story is a history lesson, so its facts are more important than its ideas'], why: 'Context helps a reader see what a story’s characters and conflicts meant in their time and place.' },
  { prompt: 'A translated story uses a word, *Dada*, that the translator leaves in the original language. Why might a translator do this?', right: 'To keep a term of respect that has no exact English equivalent', wrong: ['Because the translator forgot to translate that one word', 'To make the story harder to read for English speakers', 'Because the word is a name, so it means nothing at all'], why: 'Translators sometimes keep words that carry cultural meaning, which reminds readers they are in another culture.' },
  { prompt: 'Which is the best way to write about a cultural experience in a story from another country?', right: 'Use details from the text and its context, not sweeping claims about a people', wrong: ['Assume that the story describes every person in that country exactly as they are', 'Judge the characters by your own customs without asking about theirs', 'Ignore the setting entirely, since all people everywhere are the same'], why: 'Careful readers analyse a cultural experience through evidence, not stereotype (RL.9-10.6).' },
  { prompt: 'Two stories from different countries both show a lonely outsider in a small community. What should a comparison focus on?', right: 'How each culture shapes the outsider’s loneliness and the response to it', wrong: ['Which of the two countries is the better place to live for lonely people', 'Which of the two stories has the longer list of characters in it', 'Which writer was more famous in their own country while alive'], why: 'Comparing world literature means asking how culture shapes a shared human experience (RL.9-10.6).' },
  { prompt: 'Why does it matter that “The Postmaster” is told by a narrator who calls him “Our postmaster”?', right: 'The narrator speaks as if sharing a tale with friends, warmly but wryly', wrong: ['The narrator is the postmaster himself, telling his own life story', 'The narrator owns the post office and employs the postmaster there', 'The narrator is a villager who has never met the postmaster'], why: 'Point of view in world literature, as anywhere, shapes how we judge the characters (RL.9-10.6).' },
];

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Lesson 16.1: One subject in two mediums, and one play in many productions.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

const TRIFLES = `From Susan Glaspell, *Trifles* (1916). The men have searched the farmhouse for evidence and found none; the two women have found the evidence and hidden it. The play ends:

> COUNTY ATTORNEY: (*Facetiously*) Well, Henry, at least we found out that she was not going to quilt it. She was going to—what is it you call it, ladies?
>
> MRS. HALE: (*Hand against her pocket*) We call it—knot it, Mr. Henderson.`;

const MEDIUMS: Q[] = [
  { prompt: 'The painting *Landscape with the Fall of Icarus* (attributed to Pieter Bruegel the Elder) shows a ploughman, a shepherd and a ship in the foreground, while Icarus is only a pair of legs splashing into the sea in a corner. What does the painting **emphasize**, compared with Ovid’s myth?', right: 'The world carries on, barely noticing the boy’s fall', wrong: ['Icarus’s flight across the sky in great detail', 'The father’s grief as he searches for his son', 'The making of the wings out of wax and feathers'], why: 'The same subject can be treated very differently in two mediums; the painting shrinks the hero and enlarges the indifferent world (RL.9-10.7).' },
  { prompt: 'W. H. Auden’s poem “Musée des Beaux Arts” (1938) reflects on that painting. The poem observes that suffering happens while other people go about their ordinary business. How does the poem relate to the painting?', right: 'It draws a general idea about suffering from what the painting shows', wrong: ['It describes the painting’s colours one by one and adds nothing else', 'It retells the myth of Icarus and never mentions the painting at all', 'It argues that the painting is badly made and should be destroyed'], why: 'A poem about a painting can interpret it, turning what we see into an idea (RL.9-10.7).' },
  { prompt: 'Glaspell turned her play *Trifles* (1916) into a short story, “A Jury of Her Peers” (1917). What can the story do that the play cannot?', right: 'A narrator can describe the women’s thoughts directly', wrong: ['Actors can show the women’s feelings with their faces', 'An audience can watch the scene happen in real time', 'Lighting can darken the kitchen at the key moment'], why: 'Prose fiction can enter a character’s mind, while drama must show it through speech and action (RL.9-10.7).' },
  { prompt: 'Which is the best question for comparing a key scene in a play and in its film?', right: 'What is emphasized or left out in each, and why?', wrong: ['Which version did more people see in its first year?', 'Which version has the more famous cast of actors?', 'Which version was made more recently than the other?'], why: 'Analysing a subject in two mediums means asking what each emphasizes or omits (RL.9-10.7).' },
  { prompt: 'A poet and a painter both depict a storm at sea. Which tool belongs to the poet alone?', right: 'The sound and rhythm of words, such as crashing consonants', wrong: ['The colour and texture of the paint on the canvas', 'The size of the canvas and where it hangs in a gallery', 'The brushstrokes that show the movement of the waves'], why: 'Each medium has its own tools, so the same subject reaches us differently (RL.9-10.7).' },
  { prompt: 'Why might an illustrated edition of a poem change how readers understand it?', right: 'The pictures fix one interpretation of images the words left open', wrong: ['The pictures replace the words, so readers no longer read them', 'The pictures make the poem shorter by removing some of the lines', 'The pictures have no effect, since readers ignore them completely'], why: 'Putting a text into a second medium always involves interpretive choices (RL.9-10.7).' },
];

